ottery minus outcome in the other) across all outcome pairs, then the total sum is (3000 - 1000) + (-3000 - 1000) = -2000. This is negative. A has no such variation; its sum of differences (to B) is -2000. But the reason prefers the one with the more favorable sum. Since A has no negative outcomes, and the sum of differences is not clearly defined, the reason may be intended to evaluate the dispersion or asymmetry. However, in this case, the sum of differences (A to B) is -2000, and (B to A) is +2000. The more favorable sum would be +2000, which corresponds to B's perspective. But the reason does not specify direction. Therefore, the reason is applicable in principle — it can be applied to compute differences between outcomes of A and B — but the interpretation of 'sum of all pairwise differences' is ambiguous. Still, the necessary information (outcomes and probabilities) is available and can be used to compute pairwise differences. Thus, the decision reason can be applied to the decision problem.",
